<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Second Republic of Venezuela</h1><p class="st-meta">1813 CE – 1814 CE · capital Caracas</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The republic restored by Bolívar's Admirable Campaign of 1813, which fell within a year to the royalist llanero cavalry of José Tomás Boves.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1813 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a> invaded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/venezuela">Venezuela</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/admirable-campaign">Admirable Campaign</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-marino">Santiago Mariño</a> liberated the east. Bolívar issued the Decree of War to the Death in June 1813 and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a> in August, where he was acclaimed as the Liberator.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Bolívar</a> ruled as dictator in the west and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-marino">Mariño</a> in the east, with little formal constitution. The war became extremely brutal. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-tomas-boves">José Tomás Boves</a> raised the llaneros, plainsmen of mixed race, against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> patriots, turning the conflict into a social war. In February 1814 Bolívar ordered the execution of hundreds of Spanish prisoners at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-guaira">La Guaira</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-tomas-boves">Boves</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Bolívar</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-battle-of-la-puerta">La Puerta</a> in June 1814, and the population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a> fled east in the Emigration to the East. Boves was killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-urica">Urica</a> in December 1814, but the republic had collapsed, and Bolívar withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a> and in 1815 into exile in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jamaica">Jamaica</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/second-republic-of-venezuela.jpg" alt="Second Republic of Venezuela" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Salvadoroff · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Venezuela_(1811%E2%80%931836,_no_coat_of_arms).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1813 CE – 1814 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Caracas</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Simón Bolívar, Santiago Mariño</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/venezuela">Venezuela</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Segunda República de Venezuela</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1814&amp;focus=second-republic-of-venezuela" aria-label="Second Republic of Venezuela on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2836 1786 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Second Republic of Venezuela lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3156 2042L3138 2057M3156 2042L4910 989M3156 2042L3138 2057"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3138 2057h0M4910 989h0M3138 2057h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3156 2042h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3156 2042h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Caracas. <a href="/atlas?year=1814&amp;focus=second-republic-of-venezuela">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
